<DocsLayout title="Protocols" description="Application-layer protocols supported by RelayCore">
  <h1>Protocols</h1>

  <p>RelayCore is an L7 proxy: it parses, inspects, and rewrites application-layer protocols. Lower-layer transport is TCP (for HTTP/HTTPS/WebSocket); UDP is supported only in the transparent proxy path.</p>

  <h2>HTTP/1.1</h2>

  <p>Fully supported. Request/response headers, chunked encoding, Transfer-Encoding, bidirectional keep-alive, and streaming bodies are all parsed. Rules and scripts apply uniformly to headers and bodies.</p>

  <h2>HTTP/2</h2>

  <p>The client leg can be HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2, depending on the client and ALPN. Only an HTTP/1.x version is copied upstream. An HTTP/2 client does not force the upstream connection to HTTP/2; that leg follows the target URL and ALPN. HPACK is fully decompressed internally — rules operate on decoded pseudo-headers (<code>:method</code>, <code>:path</code>, <code>:scheme</code>, <code>:authority</code>).</p>

  <p><strong>h2c (plaintext HTTP/2) is supported too.</strong> gRPC clients that speak HTTP/2 without TLS are
    detected by their connection preface, both when they connect directly and when they tunnel through
    <code>CONNECT</code> — which is what an <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> client does. Upstreams that only speak
    h2c are reachable as well: the proxy offers HTTP/2 by prior knowledge and falls back to HTTP/1.1 if the
    upstream rejects it. Because h2c has no negotiation, that fallback waits briefly before giving up, so a
    new connection to an h2c-only upstream costs a little latency.</p>

  <h2>HTTPS / TLS</h2>

  <p>MITM interception is performed for all HTTP-over-TLS traffic using dynamically-issued certificates (see <a href="/en/docs/certificates">Certificates</a>). Upstream certificates are validated by default; in the replay endpoint you can pass <code>?accept_invalid_certs=true</code> to skip verification (debug only).</p>

  <h2>WebSocket</h2>

  <p>Handshakes and uncompressed frames can be inspected and rewritten (RFC 6455). RelayCore parses the handshake, registers a <code>Layer::WebSocket</code> flow, and exposes each message. <code>permessage-deflate</code> is neither stripped nor inflated, so compressed frames pass through opaque.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Each message records <code>direction</code> (incoming / outgoing), <code>opcode</code> (text / binary / ping / pong / close / continuation), and <code>content</code> (encoding, size, base64 body).</li>
    <li>Rules can use <code>MockWebSocketMessage</code> and <code>DropWebSocketMessage</code>.</li>
    <li>The script hook <code>onWebSocketMessage</code> can read and rewrite every message.</li>
    <li>The TUI's <strong>Messages</strong> detail tab displays the message log in real time.</li>
    <li>A body over budget emits SSE <code>body-budget-exceeded</code> with a <code>direction</code>. There is no separate WebSocket budget event name.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>QUIC / HTTP/3</h2>

  <p>QUIC runs over UDP; modern browsers prefer it when available. RelayCore controls this via <code>ProxyPolicy.quic_mode</code>:</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Downgrade</code> (default)</td><td>Strips <code>Alt-Svc</code> from the HTTP response. <code>Clear-Site-Data: "cache"</code> is added only when <code>quic_downgrade_clear_cache=true</code> (default false).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Passthrough</code></td><td>Leaves <code>Alt-Svc</code> in place. The client may then speak QUIC directly to the origin; that traffic is not proxied and produces no flow.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ExperimentalMitm</code></td><td>Experimental HTTP/3 MITM. Requires the <code>quic_mitm_experimental</code> feature flag; not yet production-ready.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>QUIC itself produces no flow. Downgrade only rewrites an HTTP response that was already captured: stripping <code>Alt-Svc</code> tags that flow <code>quic-downgraded</code>.</p>

  <h2>TCP / UDP (transparent proxy)</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>TCP</strong>: redirected traffic is parsed as HTTP/1.1. Raw TCP (non-HTTP) connections are rejected and recorded as errors.</li>
    <li><strong>UDP</strong>: forwarded via <code>--udp-tproxy-port</code> (Linux only, requires <code>IP_TRANSPARENT</code>). No parsing, just forwarding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Unsupported protocols</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Custom protocols over raw TCP</strong> (non-HTTP): RelayCore does not parse them. In transparent mode such connections will fail.</li>
    <li><strong>gRPC message payloads</strong>: the framing is parsed — a gRPC body is read as its
      length-prefixed messages (count, size, compression flag, and a text preview when the payload is
      text), and HTTP trailers are recorded, so <code>grpc-status</code> and <code>grpc-message</code>
      are visible on the flow. Decoding the protobuf fields themselves still needs the service's
      schema, which the engine does not have, so payload <em>contents</em> remain opaque.</li>
    <li><strong>SSE (Server-Sent Events)</strong>: long-lived HTTP/1.1. RelayCore uses SSE on its own <code>GET /api/v1/events</code> endpoint; external SSE traffic is recorded as plain HTTP without per-event boundaries.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
